<Project>

  <!--
    Third-party assemblies shipped by 15.9 that are signed with a 3rd party certificate.

    Main's list covers Arcade's own tooling dependencies (Wix, Octokit, AWSSDK and so on).
    15.9 ships none of those, so they are not listed here.

    THE ORIGINAL COMMENT HERE READ "even though they arrive already signed". MEASURED
    2026-09-02 AND THAT IS FALSE for the one file this rule names: Newtonsoft.Json.9.0.1
    arrives NOT signed, in all seven of its lib\* copies. The sentence is kept in this note
    rather than silently deleted, because it was the stated justification for the rule and it
    was never true.

    AND THE SHIPPED ARTIFACT PASSES THE FILE THROUGH UNTOUCHED, which is the stronger form and
    forecloses the obvious defence that the 2018 build stripped or re-signed it. Measured
    against the package as published on dotnet-public:

      AS PUBLISHED lib/net45   526,336 b  NotSigned  sha256 575E30F98E4EA42C9E516EDC...
      SHIPPED 15.9 VSIX        526,336 b  NotSigned  sha256 575E30F98E4EA42C9E516EDC...  SAME
      OURS 3064417 VSIX        536,440 b  Valid      sha256 7FB205EFC17F3622A3A4AEA6...  +10,104

    The 2018 build did not sign it and did not modify it. It shipped the bytes it was given.

    KNOWN EXPECTED DIFFERENCE FROM THE SHIPPED ARTIFACT. All four containers, measured:

      shipped Newtonsoft.Json.9.0.1.nupkg    NotSigned   all 7 lib\* copies
      shipped 15.9 VSIX                      NotSigned
      OUR nupkgs, before 2026-09-02          Valid       already deviating, unmeasured
      OUR VSIX,   before 2026-09-02          NotSigned
      OUR VSIX,   after  2026-09-02          Valid

    So this entry has ALWAYS produced a deviation - it applied to the nupkg containers from
    the beginning. Adding the VSIX to ItemsToSign did not create it; it extended it to the
    second container. Before that, our two containers DISAGREED WITH EACH OTHER about the
    same file. They now agree, and both differ from shipped.

    THE DEVIATION CANNOT BE FIXED PER-CONTAINER. FileSignInfo is global, so there is no
    configuration in which the VSIX matches shipped and the packages keep their signature.
    Matching the baseline in both places is one word - CertificateName="None" - and that is a
    signing-policy decision, not a build fix: it asks whether a Microsoft product should ship
    an unsigned third-party redistributable because the 2018 build did.

    RULED 2026-09-02: KEEP the signature. It is harmless - measured as exactly one file, a
    10,104-byte Authenticode blob, with AssemblyVersion 9.0.0.0, public key token
    30ad4fe6b2a6aeed and FileVersion 9.0.1.19813 identical on both sides. But it IS a
    difference, and a content comparison against the shipped artifacts will show
    Newtonsoft.Json.dll differing in BOTH the VSIX and the packages. That is not a regression.

    Recorded here rather than in a conversation because the person who investigates a one-file
    hash difference will be reading this file, at an hour when nobody is available to ask.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileSignInfo Include="Newtonsoft.Json.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Point ItemsToSign at where this line actually packs.

    Arcade's default glob is $(ArtifactsPackagesDir)**\*.nupkg, which RepoLayout.props:44 defines as
    artifacts\packages\$(Configuration)\. This line packs through scripts\build.ps1, which writes to
    artifacts\$(Configuration)\packages\ - the same two segments, transposed. So the default glob
    matches nothing and Sign.proj:67 fails with "List of files to sign is empty" after an otherwise
    clean compile, which is exactly what a -sign run does today.

    Declared here rather than fixed by moving or copying the packages. eng\Signing.props is Arcade's
    repo extension point and is imported by Sign.props:149, AFTER the default ItemsToSign are added
    at :62, so this is additive. Moving the pack output would change the layout that the whole
    artifact-alignment comparison is keyed on; copying would duplicate 40 MB of nupkg and create a
    second set that can drift from the first. Naming the real location does neither.

    ItemsToSign entries are containers: SignTool opens each nupkg and signs the files inside.

    THE VSIX IS LISTED FOR THE SAME REASON AND IT WAS MISSING UNTIL 2026-09-02.

    Arcade already knows how to sign one - Sign.props:88 declares
    FileExtensionSignInfo .vsix -> VsixSHA2 - but its default globs at Sign.props:55 and :63 look
    under $(VisualStudioSetupOutputPath), and this line builds its VSIX into
    artifacts\$(Configuration)\VSIX\ through scripts\build.ps1 -s Vsix. Same mismatch as the
    packages above: the glob matched nothing, and because ItemsToSign was not EMPTY the signing
    step had nothing to complain about.

    WHAT THAT SHIPPED, measured byte-for-byte on build 3064282 by comparing the SAME assembly in
    the nupkg and in the VSIX:

      nupkg copy   Authenticode Valid   sn -vf VALID   128-byte strong name signature, all non-zero
      VSIX copy    NotSigned            sn -vf FAILED  the same 128 bytes, ALL ZERO
      difference   +10,040 bytes of Authenticode blob, the PE checksum, the certificate table
                   data directory, and that 128-byte run. Nothing else.

    So the compiler emits DELAY-SIGNED assemblies and SignTool completes them when it processes a
    container. The VSIX was never a container it processed, so its payload kept the zeroed strong
    name and never received Authenticode. 18 repo-built product binaries and 182 satellites were
    affected.

    It was not noticed because the payload RUNS: .NET Framework bypasses strong-name verification
    for full-trust assemblies, so the unsigned VSIX discovered and executed tests correctly. A
    working artifact is not evidence of a signed one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\**\*.nupkg" />
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\**\*.vsix" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Do not sign .js, and do not treat their presence as an error.

    Modern Arcade stopped signing .js by default and raises an error for each one it finds inside a
    container, telling the repo to either declare FileExtensionSignInfo for .js or set NoSignJS. On
    this line that fires four times, on IntelliTrace WebViews content:

      CallDurationView.js   plugin.js   JSTreeGridControl.js   MicrosoftAjax-4.0.0.0.debug.js

    All four are CARRIED payload and all four are byte-identical to their counterparts in the
    shipped 15.9.2 packages - measured, not assumed. So the shipped artifact does not carry a
    signature on them either, and NOT signing them is what reproduces what shipped.

    Chosen over declaring FileExtensionSignInfo for .js because that would sign files the shipped
    artifact left unsigned, which changes the artifact to satisfy the tool. The setting that matches
    the baseline wins over the setting that looks more thorough.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoSignJS>true</NoSignJS>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    DO NOT SIGN .cab. THE SAME RULE AS .js ABOVE, AND UNLIKE .js IT FAILED SILENTLY.

    Arcade 11 signs .cab by default - Sign.props:86 declares

      <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".dll;.exe;.mibc;.msi;.cab" CertificateName="Microsoft400" />

    and Arcade 9 had no .cab entry at all. This repo declares no FileExtensionSignInfo, so .cab fell
    through to that default and the one cab in the product was re-signed.

    MEASURED on build 3065146's delivered Microsoft.TestPlatform package, against the shipped
    15.9.2 nupkg and against this build's own INPUT:

      input   staged-payload Microsoft.Internal.Intellitrace 15.9.0-preview-20180924-02
              IntelliTraceCollection.cab  2,429,204 bytes  SHA CB358CC3...  signer CN=VS Bld Lab
      shipped same 2,429,204 bytes, same SHA CB358CC3...  - byte-identical to the input
      built   2,432,100 bytes  SHA 037102A6...  signer CN=Microsoft Corporation,
              certificate serial 330000021CFACDC59BE82F7EC400000000021C, notBefore 2026-04-16

    So the input was already correctly signed by VS Bld Lab in 2017, shipped that way, and this
    pipeline replaced that signature with a 2026 certificate. The build was GREEN.

    WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT. Of the 421 files present in both the built and the shipped package, 286
    differ - but 267 .dll and 15 .exe differ because this build compiles them at a new version. The
    cab is the ONLY file the build does not compile that changed. Every check this line ran until
    now compared PATHS or package ENTRY LISTS, and a path comparison cannot see a file whose name is
    identical and whose bytes are not. An entry list reported this package correct.

    The asymmetry that makes this class dangerous, and the reason it is written here rather than in
    a note: a DECLARED extension colliding with an Arcade default is a hard build error nobody can
    miss, while an UNDECLARED extension picking up a new default is a silent content change on a
    green build. The loud half was fixed on a sibling line in minutes; this half shipped.

    Remove-then-re-add rather than deletion, for the reason recorded above for .js: the goal is the
    bytes the product shipped, not the setting that looks more thorough.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Remove=".cab" />
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".cab" CertificateName="None" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Assert the resolved ItemsToSign SET against the packages actually produced, BY NAME.

    Sign.proj:67 errors when ItemsToSign is entirely empty, and that is the good case - it is loud.
    The dangerous case is a glob that matches SOME of them: signing then exits 0 having signed a
    subset, and the packages that were skipped are indistinguishable from the ones that were signed
    until someone opens them. An unsigned assembly inside a signed-looking package is exactly the
    failure this whole effort exists to prevent.

    So this compares two sets and names every discrepancy. It never reports a count as the verdict:
    "9 items" is true of the right nine and of any other nine, and every count-based check on this
    project has eventually concealed a compensating pair.

    Runs before Execute, so a mismatch stops the build before anything is submitted for signing.
  -->
  <Target Name="AssertSigningSetMatchesProducedPackages" BeforeTargets="Execute">

    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        DELIBERATELY NOT the same glob text as ItemsToSign above.

        A mutation test caught this: narrowing "packages\**\*.nupkg" narrowed BOTH sides at once, so
        the comparison found them equal and passed while signing only one package of nine. Two sides
        of a comparison that share a source of truth cannot disagree, which makes the check vacuous
        in exactly the case it exists for.

        Written with a distinct pattern so a single edit cannot silently change both. If the
        DIRECTORY is wrong rather than the pattern, both sides do go empty together - and that case
        is caught by the empty-population Error below, which is why that Error is not optional.
      -->
      <_ProducedPackage Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\*.nupkg;$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\*\*.nupkg" />

      <!--
        The VSIX belongs on this side too, and its absence is why this assertion could not catch
        the unsigned VSIX shipped up to 2026-09-02.

        Both sides of the comparison enumerated NUPKGS ONLY. ItemsToSign listed nine nupkgs, the
        produced set found the same nine, the sets matched exactly, and the check passed - while a
        17 MB VSIX full of delay-signed assemblies sat beside them, in neither set.

        AN AGREEMENT BETWEEN TWO INCLUSION LISTS SAYS NOTHING ABOUT A CATEGORY THAT IS ABSENT FROM
        BOTH. This check was never wrong about what it compared; it was silent about what it did
        not enumerate, which is the harder failure to see because it produces a clean pass rather
        than a wrong answer.
      -->
      <_ProducedPackage Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\*.vsix;$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\*\*.vsix" />
      <_SigningSetNames Include="@(ItemsToSign->'%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
      <_ProducedNames Include="@(_ProducedPackage->'%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- Both directions, separately, never netted against each other. -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <_MissingFromSigningSet Include="@(_ProducedNames)" Exclude="@(_SigningSetNames)" />
      <_UnexpectedInSigningSet Include="@(_SigningSetNames)" Exclude="@(_ProducedNames)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      An empty produced set would make both differences empty and this check vacuously green - the
      same "match over an empty comparison set" defect that a match count without its population
      hides. Assert the population before asserting the comparison.
    -->
    <Error Condition="'@(_ProducedPackage)' == ''"
           Text="Signing set assertion cannot run: no packages found under $(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\ and no vsix under $(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\. This is NOT a pass - the comparison had nothing on one side." />

    <!--
      Assert each CATEGORY is non-empty, not just their union.

      The union being non-empty is satisfied by nine nupkgs and zero vsix, which is exactly the
      state that shipped an unsigned VSIX while every check reported green. A category that
      disappears entirely cannot be caught by a check on the total.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ProducedNupkg Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\**\*.nupkg" />
      <_ProducedVsix Include="$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\**\*.vsix" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(_ProducedNupkg)' == ''"
           Text="No .nupkg was produced under $(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\packages\. The signing-set comparison would still pass on the vsix alone, so this is asserted separately." />

    <Error Condition="'@(_ProducedVsix)' == ''"
           Text="No .vsix was produced under $(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\VSIX\. The signing-set comparison would still pass on the packages alone - which is precisely how an unsigned VSIX shipped from this line until 2026-09-02." />

    <Error Condition="'@(_MissingFromSigningSet)' != ''"
           Text="These produced packages are NOT in ItemsToSign and would ship unsigned: @(_MissingFromSigningSet, ', ')" />

    <Error Condition="'@(_UnexpectedInSigningSet)' != ''"
           Text="ItemsToSign names entries that were not produced here: @(_UnexpectedInSigningSet, ', ')" />

    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Signing set verified by name: @(_ProducedNames->Count()) produced, all present in ItemsToSign - @(_ProducedNames, ', ')" />
  </Target>

</Project>
